Starting Synchronism Explorer Session at Thu Oct 29 08:00:01 PDT 2026
TEST-10's rotation-curve exclusion holds up once SPARC's distance, inclination and M/L errors are propagated. TEST-10 is the one row in the count floor that refutes the framework's own content on data. What it actually depends on is the gas census, not those error bars.

I worked the topic the maintainer pointed to (`test10-robust-half-…`), because it carries the site's minimum claim and a researcher persona asked it directly: "is the robust exclusion one galaxy or many?" I pre-registered the test (commit d38045c) before running it. In WAKE I added a gas-mass nuisance to the registration, in case the binding uncertainty wasn't distance or inclination. It turned out to be the gas.

**Results at the permissive cap B ≤ 6.39** (fitting each galaxy with distance, inclination, M/L and gas free under their priors):
- **NGC 3741:** distance and inclination barely matter (χ² = 82 with those plus M/L free). Allowing the gas mass to vary drops it to χ² = 9.5, and doubling its gas would remove the exclusion.
- **ESO 444-G084:** it falls to 1.65σ once its 32 ± 6° inclination is propagated. The ledger's "M/L-free pair NGC 3741 / ESO 444-G084" is half wrong.
- **NGC 2915:** the site calls it "M/L-conditional", but escaping needs M/L ≥ 2.2 at 3.6 μm, about 6σ above the prior. It is the most robust galaxy once all the uncertainties are combined.
- **Count:** of the 28 face-value exceeders, 6 survive at 2σ and 3 at 3σ or more (NGC 2915, NGC 3741, UGC 06667). Three more galaxies that pass at the outer radii fail further in.
- **Predictions:** two of three confirmed and one half confirmed; ESO 444-G084 survives narrowly when gas is held fixed.

**Follow-up (not pre-registered; rule fixed in the script before running):** the escape that remains is extra unseen gas, a factor of 2–3 proposed in the literature (Pfenniger & Revaz 2005). If such gas were the same for every galaxy, gas-rich galaxies would sit off the baryonic Tully–Fisher relation. They don't: the extra gas is limited to ×1.25 or less at M/L 0.5 and ×1.75 or less at 0.7 (95% galaxy bootstrap). So saving NGC 3741 would need about ×2 gas and M/L ≳ 0.8 at the same time, and NGC 2915's ×3.1 is excluded at every defensible M/L. The only escape left is hidden gas that differs from galaxy to galaxy.

**For the maintainer:** the finding has replacement wording for the TEST-10 card and the count-floor box on /honest-assessment. That includes dropping the "upper bounds on significance" caveat, since distance and inclination are now propagated. The literature citations come from search summaries, not full papers, and I didn't get Gentile+2007's own gas factor for NGC 3741.

I committed and pushed (0b8a7b5), archived the topic, and saved one memory: report the actual M/L or gas value needed before calling a result "conditional".

